            "heading": "Captive Bead Rings (BCR's)",
            "show_category_card": true
          }
        },
        "question_HrDmCB": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are captive bead rings?",
            "answer": "<p>Captive bead rings, also known as <strong>CBRs</strong> or <strong>ball closure rings</strong>, are circular pieces of body jewellery that use a small removable bead or ball to close the ring securely. The tension of the ring holds the bead in place, creating a seamless, secure look.</p>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "question_BcwP4T": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What’s the difference between clicker rings and standard captive bead rings?",
            "answer": "<ul><li><strong>Clicker rings</strong> feature a hinge and clasp mechanism that “clicks” into place. They are easy to open and close without tools, making them ideal for frequent wear or jewellery changes.</li><li><strong>Standard captive bead rings</strong> use a small bead that fits tightly between the ends of the ring and is held in place by pressure. They’re more traditional and often preferred for their simplicity and secure fit</li></ul>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "question_faxdQ6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": " How do I insert or remove a captive bead ring?",
            "answer": "<ul><li>For <strong>clicker rings</strong>,<br/>simply open the hinged section, insert the ring into your piercing, and click it shut until you hear it snap into place.</li><li>For <strong>standard captive bead rings</strong>, gently separate the ring ends just enough to release or replace the bead. Ring-opening pliers can be used for thicker gauges to make the process easier.</li></ul>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "question_cNX77G": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What piercings can captive bead rings be used for?",
            "answer": "<p>Captive bead rings and clickers can be used in many piercings including <strong>nose, ear, lip, septum, eyebrow, daith, rook, conch, tragus, and nipple piercings</strong>. The versatility of the design makes them one of the most adaptable jewellery types available.</p>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        }
